% ============================================================================
% respiratory-parts — each part of the respiratory system and the function /
% role its source explicitly states for it (adj-facts-stdlib, ANATOMY).
% ============================================================================
% Air does not just fall into the blood; it is handed down a chain of parts,
% each with ONE job the anatomy books state out loud. The nose traps the big
% particles; the pharynx lets the air pass through; the larynx does speech; the
% trachea is the main airway; the bronchi branch down to the air sacs; the lungs
% are where gases are exchanged with the blood (external respiration); the
% alveoli are the actual gas-exchange surface; and the diaphragm contracts to
% pull the breath in. Before a MEDICINE agent can reason about a cough, a
% collapsed lung, or a blocked airway it must first know WHICH part does WHICH
% job. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? part_function(trachea, $Function)      % main_airway
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `part_function(part, function)` carrying the table's citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the stated
% function WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a
% word that is not one of these respiratory parts (it never invents a function).
%
% Each part of the respiratory system and the function / role its source states
% for it, as a little truth table (roughly nose-to-alveoli, then the muscle that
% drives the whole thing):
%
%     respiratory part   stated function / role   a beginner's cue
%     ----------------   ----------------------   ----------------------------
%     nose               traps_particles          nose hairs trap inhaled dust
%     pharynx            passes_air               the throat lets air pass through
%     larynx             human_speech             the voice box; speech
%     trachea            main_airway              the windpipe to the lungs
%     bronchi            branch_to_alveoli        branch down to the air sacs
%     lungs              external_respiration     gases swap with the blood here
%     alveoli            gas_exchange             the actual air-to-blood surface
%     diaphragm          contracts_inspiration    contracts to pull the breath in
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a function and recall the part that does
% it:
%
%     ? part_function($Part, gas_exchange)   % alveoli
%
% A word that is NOT one of these respiratory parts — `stomach`, `heart`,
% `femur` — is deliberately NOT a row, so a part recall ABSTAINS on it rather
% than inventing a function. That honest-abstention property is what makes the
% table safe to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the part→function pairs
% it can ground, and only those.
%
% The `function` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be a
% role the source EXPLICITLY states for that part — never one the source does
% not support. Where the everyday word differs from the source's word — the
% source says the trachea is the "main airway", not that it "carries air"; it
% says the diaphragm "contracts" during "inspiration", not that it "controls
% breathing" — the atom uses the source's own word, so every value stays
% literally checkable against the quoted span.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every part→function pair
% below was read out of a fetched NCI SEER Training Modules page, and any pair
% whose function could not be verified there would have been dropped). Each
% pair, with the primary source it was copied from and the verbatim span that
% states it:
%
%   nose → traps_particles
%     https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/respiratory/passages/nose.html
%     (NCI SEER Training Modules — U.S. National Cancer Institute)
%     "Nose hairs at the entrance to the nose trap large inhaled particles."
%
%   pharynx → passes_air
%     https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/respiratory/passages/pharynx.html
%     (NCI SEER Training Modules — U.S. National Cancer Institute)
%     "The upper part of the pharynx (throat) lets only air pass through."
%
%   larynx → human_speech
%     https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/respiratory/passages/larynx.html
%     (NCI SEER Training Modules — U.S. National Cancer Institute)
%     "The larynx plays an essential role in human speech."
%
%   trachea → main_airway
%     https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/respiratory/passages/larynx.html
%     (NCI SEER Training Modules — U.S. National Cancer Institute)
%     "The trachea, commonly called the windpipe, is the main airway to the
%      lungs."
%
%   bronchi → branch_to_alveoli
%     https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/respiratory/passages/bronchi.html
%     (NCI SEER Training Modules — U.S. National Cancer Institute)
%     "The bronchi branch into smaller and smaller passageways until they
%      terminate in tiny air sacs called alveoli."
%
%   lungs → external_respiration
%     https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/respiratory/
%     (NCI SEER Training Modules — U.S. National Cancer Institute)
%     "After this, there is an exchange of gases between the lungs and the
%      blood. This is called external respiration."
%
%   alveoli → gas_exchange
%     https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/respiratory/passages/bronchi.html
%     (NCI SEER Training Modules — U.S. National Cancer Institute)
%     "Exchange of gases between the air in the lungs and the blood in the
%      capillaries occurs across the walls of the alveolar ducts and alveoli."
%
%   diaphragm → contracts_inspiration
%     https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/respiratory/mechanics.html
%     (NCI SEER Training Modules — U.S. National Cancer Institute)
%     "During inspiration, the diaphragm contracts and the thoracic cavity
%      increases in volume."
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the SEER statement that fixes the
% first row (nose → traps_particles). Every OTHER row's per-fact source and
% verbatim span is listed above, so each function remains independently
% checkable. All sources are the NCI SEER Training Modules, a primary U.S.
% government (National Cancer Institute) teaching resource, so `trust
% authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table part_function {
    columns part, function

    row (nose, traps_particles)
    row (pharynx, passes_air)
    row (larynx, human_speech)
    row (trachea, main_airway)
    row (bronchi, branch_to_alveoli)
    row (lungs, external_respiration)
    row (alveoli, gas_exchange)
    row (diaphragm, contracts_inspiration)

    source "Nose hairs at the entrance to the nose trap large inhaled particles."
    locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/respiratory/passages/nose.html"
    trust authoritative
}
